{"code":"\"\"\"Error types for minidb.\n\nThis module holds the single public exception of the package. It depends on\nnothing else, so every other module may import it without creating a cycle.\n\"\"\"\n\n__all__ = [\"QueryError\"]\n\n\nclass QueryError(Exception):\n    \"\"\"Raised for any query that minidb cannot execute.\n\n    This covers malformed SQL, unknown tables or columns, ambiguous column\n    references, mixed-type comparisons, negative LIMIT/OFFSET values and type\n    errors. It is the only exception the public API is allowed to raise, so\n    callers branch on the exception type rather than on the message text.\n    \"\"\"\n","concerns":["The peer review reports no defect in errors.py; every finding concerns parser.py, planner.py or engine.py, so this file is unchanged from round 1 and still exports QueryError and nothing else, exactly as published.","Most reported failures (planner reading node fields such as `select.from_table`, `ref.name` or `expr.args`, and indexing a non-mapping `tables`) would escape as AttributeError or TypeError. errors.py cannot intercept them: QueryError is a plain Exception with no hooks or registry, so api.query's `except Exception -> raise QueryError(...) from exc` stays the only conversion point, and the modules that detect a bad node or a bad argument shape must raise QueryError themselves.","No error-code enum, severity field, factory helper or exception subclass is added, because dependent modules were written against `raise QueryError(<message>)` with an opaque message and branch only on the exception type."],"exports":["QueryError"],"path":"minidb/errors.py"}